WEBVTT

00:00:00.987 --> 00:00:01.954
This is a frog.

00:00:01.954 --> 00:00:04.837
Our network has to figure that out from nothing but numbers.

00:00:04.949 --> 00:00:09.913
CIFAR-10 is 60,000 tiny photos in ten classes, from airplanes to trucks.

00:00:09.913 --> 00:00:13.517
50,000 for training, and 10,000 held out for testing.

00:00:13.726 --> 00:00:15.845
Zoom in and the frog disappears.

00:00:15.845 --> 00:00:21.780
Each pixel is three numbers, red, green, and blue, from 0 to 255.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.878
32 by 32 by 3.

00:00:23.878 --> 00:00:26.023
3,072 numbers.

00:00:26.289 --> 00:00:28.224
So, what actually makes a network better at

00:00:28.224 --> 00:00:30.283
turning those numbers into the right answer?

00:00:32.219 --> 00:00:34.654
We started with a small convolutional network.

00:00:34.654 --> 00:00:38.205
545,098 trainable parameters.

00:00:38.463 --> 00:00:39.920
First, normalization.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.540
Each color channel has its dataset mean subtracted,

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.251
then gets divided by its spread.

00:00:45.027 --> 00:00:46.429
Then, convolution.

00:00:46.429 --> 00:00:49.951
A filter is a 3 by 3 window through all three channels.

00:00:49.951 --> 00:00:52.168
27 weights, plus a bias.

00:00:52.168 --> 00:00:54.886
At each position, it multiplies and sums.

00:00:54.886 --> 00:00:57.205
Slide it everywhere, and you get a new map.

00:00:57.482 --> 00:00:59.895
The same weights are reused at every position,

00:00:59.895 --> 00:01:03.409
so a pattern only has to be learned once, wherever it shows up.

00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:06.476
With 32 filters, we get 32 maps.

00:01:06.476 --> 00:01:08.726
These are the real filters our baseline learned,

00:01:08.726 --> 00:01:10.552
and their real maps for this frog.

00:01:10.552 --> 00:01:12.617
Nobody hardcoded edge detectors.

00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:13.744
Training found these.

00:01:14.029 --> 00:01:17.594
ReLU zeroes the negatives, and 2 by 2 max pooling keeps

00:01:17.594 --> 00:01:22.270
each block's strongest value, 32 by 32 down to 16 by 16.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.903
The second block repeats this with 64 filters reading all 32 maps,

00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:28.990
combining simple patterns into parts.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:30.662
Pooling takes it to 8 by 8.

00:01:30.865 --> 00:01:33.126
That's 4,096 numbers.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:36.170
Flatten them, 128 hidden units with ReLU,

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.664
and out come ten scores, one per class.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.074
Those ten scores are logits.

00:01:42.074 --> 00:01:44.302
Any size, positive or negative.

00:01:44.302 --> 00:01:45.665
Not probabilities yet.

00:01:45.851 --> 00:01:48.601
Softmax turns them into probabilities.

00:01:48.601 --> 00:01:51.688
Exponentiate each one, then divide by the total.

00:01:51.688 --> 00:01:53.685
Now they're positive, and they sum to one.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.384
For this frog, the baseline bets mostly on frog, but not all in.

00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:00.357
Cross-entropy is the negative log of

00:02:00.357 --> 00:02:02.367
the probability on the right answer.

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:04.596
Confident and right costs almost nothing.

00:02:04.596 --> 00:02:06.539
Confident and wrong costs a lot.

00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:08.313
One detail.

00:02:08.313 --> 00:02:10.694
The network itself only outputs logits.

00:02:10.694 --> 00:02:13.966
PyTorch's cross-entropy applies log-softmax internally.

00:02:13.966 --> 00:02:17.224
Softmax here is for reading outputs, not an extra layer.

00:02:17.518 --> 00:02:20.823
Backpropagation uses the chain rule to send that loss backwards,

00:02:20.823 --> 00:02:23.181
telling every parameter which way would lower it.

00:02:23.951 --> 00:02:27.201
This is a real slice of the loss around our trained baseline,

00:02:27.201 --> 00:02:29.788
along two random directions in weight space.

00:02:29.788 --> 00:02:31.516
Gradient descent steps downhill.

00:02:31.516 --> 00:02:34.665
The path drawn here is illustrative, not recorded steps.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:38.069
Each step uses a batch of 64 images.

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:42.323
One pass over the training set, an epoch, is 782 steps.

00:02:42.433 --> 00:02:46.407
Ten epochs at learning rate 0.01 gave our first baseline.

00:02:46.407 --> 00:02:47.983
63.68 percent on test.

00:02:47.983 --> 00:02:50.450
Wrong more than a third of the time.

00:02:52.298 --> 00:02:54.535
First, we tried going faster.

00:02:54.535 --> 00:02:58.563
At batch 512, ten epochs took 5 seconds instead of 22.

00:02:58.673 --> 00:03:01.657
But accuracy fell to 48.58 percent.

00:03:01.657 --> 00:03:05.221
Turns out bigger batches mean fewer steps.

00:03:05.221 --> 00:03:07.625
980 updates instead of 7,820.

00:03:07.823 --> 00:03:10.734
With 80 epochs, 7,840 updates,

00:03:10.734 --> 00:03:15.336
almost exactly matched, it reached 67.67 percent.

00:03:15.546 --> 00:03:18.122
But each step used eight times as many images.

00:03:18.122 --> 00:03:19.546
Bigger batches aren't magic.

00:03:19.546 --> 00:03:21.953
They trade more work per step for fewer steps.

00:03:23.889 --> 00:03:25.313
Next, augmentation.

00:03:25.313 --> 00:03:28.715
Show a variation of each photo instead of the exact same one.

00:03:28.715 --> 00:03:31.808
Flip it, or pad the border and crop a shifted window.

00:03:32.013 --> 00:03:34.035
A horse facing left is still a horse.

00:03:34.035 --> 00:03:36.394
This teaches invariance, and makes memorizing

00:03:36.394 --> 00:03:39.511
single images harder, a form of regularization.

00:03:39.627 --> 00:03:45.163
We tested 15 recipes, three seeds each, on the small network.

00:03:45.163 --> 00:03:48.020
No augmentation, 67.96 percent.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.609
Flip helped a little, 68.77.

00:03:50.609 --> 00:03:53.020
Crop plus flip hurt, 66.38.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:54.267
The subtle part.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:58.539
Crop plus flip shrank the train-test gap from about 7 points to under 1.

00:03:58.539 --> 00:04:01.387
But a smaller gap can mean useful regularization,

00:04:01.387 --> 00:04:03.137
or a model that just learned less.

00:04:03.137 --> 00:04:07.061
Test accuracy fell, so the gap alone isn't evidence of improvement.

00:04:07.352 --> 00:04:09.917
This suite also checked the test set repeatedly,

00:04:09.917 --> 00:04:11.743
so treat it as exploratory.

00:04:13.695 --> 00:04:14.782
Then we got stricter.

00:04:14.782 --> 00:04:18.217
We held out a stratified validation set of 5,000 images,

00:04:18.217 --> 00:04:22.250
trained on 45,000, and made every decision on validation.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:24.206
Test was reserved for final assessment,

00:04:24.206 --> 00:04:27.662
but the earlier suite had used it, so it's not a pristine holdout.

00:04:27.943 --> 00:04:29.074
And a new recipe.

00:04:29.074 --> 00:04:34.096
Batch 128, SGD with momentum and weight decay, a warmup then cosine

00:04:34.096 --> 00:04:38.657
learning rate over 160 epochs, and batch norm after every convolution.

00:04:38.931 --> 00:04:41.105
Every setup runs three seeds.

00:04:41.105 --> 00:04:43.583
Scores average the last ten validation epochs,

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:47.101
not the luckiest one, and plus or minus is the seed spread.

00:04:47.397 --> 00:04:50.331
The same two-convolution design, plus batch norm,

00:04:50.331 --> 00:04:56.484
now scores 78.27 on validation, versus 63.68 on test before.

00:04:56.484 --> 00:04:59.799
But the split, the metric, and several knobs all changed,

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.699
so that's not an isolated ablation.

00:05:03.631 --> 00:05:05.685
Now, what if we stack more convolutions?

00:05:05.685 --> 00:05:07.914
Same stages, pooling, and head.

00:05:07.914 --> 00:05:08.897
Just more layers.

00:05:09.104 --> 00:05:13.625
Each extra 3 by 3 layer widens what a unit can see, 5 by 5,

00:05:13.625 --> 00:05:17.201
then 7 by 7, and lets it compose more complex functions.

00:05:17.402 --> 00:05:20.228
Two convolutions, 78.27.

00:05:20.228 --> 00:05:22.174
Four, 84.53.

00:05:22.174 --> 00:05:24.348
Eight, 86.90.

00:05:24.348 --> 00:05:26.815
Sixteen, 87.17.

00:05:26.815 --> 00:05:28.826
Smaller gains, but still going up.

00:05:28.826 --> 00:05:32.305
Then at 32, it drops to 83.08.

00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:36.035
Every network fits its training labels, almost 100 percent.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:37.884
But fitting isn't generalizing.

00:05:37.884 --> 00:05:40.889
These results alone can't say whether optimization,

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:43.200
generalization, or both caused the drop.

00:05:43.468 --> 00:05:45.555
And unlike the original ResNet paper,

00:05:45.555 --> 00:05:48.783
where deeper plain nets had higher training error, ours didn't.

00:05:48.783 --> 00:05:52.286
Residuals are a plausible fix to test, not a diagnosis.

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:57.014
A residual block keeps the same two convolutions,

00:05:57.014 --> 00:06:00.791
but adds a shortcut that skips around them and gets added back at the end.

00:06:00.975 --> 00:06:02.856
So the block computes F of x.

00:06:02.856 --> 00:06:07.030
Conv, batch norm, ReLU, conv, batch norm.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.465
And the output is ReLU of F of x, plus the shortcut.

00:06:11.334 --> 00:06:14.617
When channels grow, our shortcut is padded with zeros.

00:06:14.617 --> 00:06:16.889
No learned weights, so each residual network

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:19.744
matches its plain twin's parameters exactly.

00:06:19.959 --> 00:06:20.916
Why might that help?

00:06:20.916 --> 00:06:24.448
A block that should do nearly nothing just pushes F toward zero,

00:06:24.448 --> 00:06:26.394
instead of learning to copy its input.

00:06:26.394 --> 00:06:29.764
And in backprop, the shortcuts give gradients a direct route.

00:06:29.764 --> 00:06:31.238
Schematic, not measured.

00:06:31.523 --> 00:06:33.979
Matched seeds, identical starting weights.

00:06:33.979 --> 00:06:36.903
Residual 4, 83.77.

00:06:36.903 --> 00:06:38.903
Eight, 86.67.

00:06:38.903 --> 00:06:41.099
Sixteen, 86.85.

00:06:41.099 --> 00:06:43.628
Thirty-two, 87.20.

00:06:43.734 --> 00:06:48.708
At 32 layers, the shortcut matters, 83.08 up to 87.20.

00:06:48.708 --> 00:06:52.415
But it didn't beat plain at 4, 8, or 16,

00:06:52.415 --> 00:06:58.383
and it's only 0.03 points above plain 16, inside the seed spread.

00:06:58.383 --> 00:07:03.175
On test, it scored 86.43 percent, plus or minus 0.49.

00:07:05.121 --> 00:07:06.121
Now the twist.

00:07:06.121 --> 00:07:10.147
Crop plus flip, which hurt the tiny network, on residual 32.

00:07:10.354 --> 00:07:13.224
No augmentation, 87.20.

00:07:13.224 --> 00:07:15.017
Flip, 90.31.

00:07:15.017 --> 00:07:17.854
Crop plus flip, 93.74.

00:07:17.854 --> 00:07:21.332
About six and a half points, and it held for all three seeds.

00:07:21.603 --> 00:07:23.766
Same augmentation, opposite result.

00:07:23.766 --> 00:07:27.603
Plausibly, a bigger model with a stronger recipe can use that variety.

00:07:27.603 --> 00:07:31.635
We didn't prove that, and model, recipe, and split all changed together.

00:07:31.635 --> 00:07:33.852
A technique's value depends on what it's paired with.

00:07:35.848 --> 00:07:40.924
Finally, ResNet-18, adapted for CIFAR and trained from random weights.

00:07:40.924 --> 00:07:42.403
No ImageNet pre-training.

00:07:42.603 --> 00:07:47.592
The stem is one 3 by 3 convolution, 3 to 64 channels, stride 1.

00:07:47.592 --> 00:07:51.810
ImageNet's 7 by 7 stride 2 stem and max pool would crush a 32

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:55.970
by 32 image to 8 by 8, so we swapped one and dropped the other.

00:07:56.174 --> 00:07:58.141
Then eight basic residual blocks,

00:07:58.141 --> 00:08:04.184
two per stage, at 64, 128, 256, and 512 channels.

00:08:04.184 --> 00:08:05.858
Each stage halves resolution.

00:08:05.858 --> 00:08:08.706
32, 16, 8, 4.

00:08:08.706 --> 00:08:10.478
Where shapes change, the shortcut is a

00:08:10.478 --> 00:08:13.343
learned 1 by 1 convolution with batch norm.

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:17.505
Global average pooling turns each 4 by 4 map into one number,

00:08:17.505 --> 00:08:21.385
and one linear layer maps 512 features to ten classes.

00:08:21.385 --> 00:08:23.158
No hidden 128 layer.

00:08:23.347 --> 00:08:24.271
Why 18?

00:08:24.271 --> 00:08:29.347
The stem plus 16 block convolutions is 17, and the classifier makes 18.

00:08:29.347 --> 00:08:31.997
By convention, shortcut projections don't count.

00:08:32.291 --> 00:08:33.704
So it's not just layer count.

00:08:33.704 --> 00:08:36.639
It keeps resolution longer, has far more channels,

00:08:36.639 --> 00:08:41.910
about 11.2 million parameters, nine times residual 32, and pools globally.

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:44.878
And it trained 200 epochs, not 160.

00:08:44.878 --> 00:08:47.770
A recipe comparison, not a clean architecture test.

00:08:47.963 --> 00:08:51.881
Across three seeds, it averaged 95.77 percent validation

00:08:51.881 --> 00:08:55.662
accuracy over the last ten epochs, plus or minus 0.11.

00:08:55.662 --> 00:08:59.580
We score the final ten epochs, never the single best one.

00:09:01.437 --> 00:09:04.461
The queue has now finished and selected

00:09:04.461 --> 00:09:07.864
ResNet-18 with crop plus flip by validation.

00:09:07.864 --> 00:09:12.477
Its final test accuracy is 95.21 percent, plus or minus 0.02.

00:09:12.761 --> 00:09:13.728
The whole path.

00:09:13.728 --> 00:09:16.750
63.68 for the classroom baseline.

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:19.457
78.27 with a stricter recipe.

00:09:19.457 --> 00:09:22.424
87.20 with depth and shortcuts.

00:09:22.424 --> 00:09:25.141
93.74 with augmentation.

00:09:25.141 --> 00:09:28.294
95.77 with ResNet-18.

00:09:28.294 --> 00:09:32.091
Test and validation are mixed, so it's a story, not a leaderboard.

00:09:32.357 --> 00:09:35.998
It's the strongest we've measured, not the best ResNet that exists.

00:09:35.998 --> 00:09:39.118
Wider and deeper variants and other recipes do better.

00:09:39.118 --> 00:09:40.503
This isn't state of the art.

00:09:40.779 --> 00:09:42.236
The lessons generalize.

00:09:42.236 --> 00:09:44.638
Count optimizer steps, not just epochs.

00:09:44.638 --> 00:09:46.910
Decide on validation, across seeds.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.105
A smaller gap isn't better performance.

00:09:49.105 --> 00:09:51.722
And a technique's value depends on everything around it.

00:09:52.006 --> 00:09:53.527
No single trick did this.

00:09:53.527 --> 00:09:56.335
It was the design and the recipe, working together.
